	(text_box "MIC2288 is a PWM DC-DC boost converter IC. FB adjusts the duty cycle of PWM.\n- Operating frequency of 1.2MHz \n- Input 2.5V-10V\n- Output up to 34V"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 32.512 153.416 0)
		(size 82.55 9.906)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "11149e23-702a-4fce-9fde-125125e69dfb")
	)
	(text_box "Bootstrapping properly biases highside gate.\n- Capacitor is charged to VCC when highside off, lowside on\n- highside on, lowside off, internal level shifter adjust path and capacitor serves to boost voltage such that V_GS turns MOSFET on"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 249.174 74.93 0)
		(size 76.454 10.668)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.1588)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "18d3a540-13d9-4b21-a5a3-c42bf612fe2c")
	)
	(text_box "This is a rather common design for Boost Converters, with most having an efficiency of ~75% depending on component quality.\nLosses in this design can be found in: \n- MOSFET conduction and switching loss\n- Diode forward-voltage loss\n- Component ESR\n- Feedback resistor divider\nWith severity of loss getting less and less critical as we move down. Some losses are already minimized thanks to optimized ICs such as the MOSFETS losses, but other portions may have real impact."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 24.638 167.386 0)
		(size 122.936 20.574)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.1588)
				(color 255 33 69 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "3fdb3106-9b71-4371-adea-e5016a73994a")
	)
	(text_box "RES - restart time pion for external cap that configures hiccup mode. This mode allows device to continually restart if a fault is detected in hopes that it clears itself. This protects the device from overheating and keeps components intact.\n\nSS - Soft Start pin. External capacitor sets ramp time of internal error amp. "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 251.714 184.912 0)
		(size 52.832 18.796)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "44bfe008-512f-413b-a69c-dbc7bcb47606")
	)
	(text_box "Diode prevents capacitors from discharging to GND in off cycles."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 76.708 101.092 0)
		(size 35.56 5.588)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "7b7fccd7-2d41-442a-a2f8-608195e81469")
	)
	(text_box "FB (Feedback) operates by maintaining a 1.2V threshold. \n- >1.2V causes output to decrease\n- <1.2V cause output to increase\n- Internal Error Amp compared 1.2V reference to FB voltage\n- Values for divider circuit are based on an output range of 12V-48V.\n\nComp is output of internal error amplifer. Connected loop compression network between this pin and FB pin\n\nLoop Compression Network is a group of resistors/capacitors that stabilize feedback loop of SMPS. Ensures converter reaches desired output voltage quickly without overshoot/oscillating.\nCapacitor and Resistor create frequency dependent feedback path. At DC capacitor blocks current, but amplifer still regulates normally. At higher frequencies, capacitor conducts, resistor limits amount of feedback, together they shape amps gain/phase.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 311.658 177.292 0)
		(size 76.454 34.798)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.1588)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "a3e127a8-25f1-445d-ac89-443fb11ad270")
	)
	(text_box "Inductor builds magnetic fields / stores current potential. Primary factor in boosting voltage.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 22.352 101.092 0)
		(size 49.784 5.588)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "baf8ad59-bba5-48b0-a107-91fcbb22f3fa")
	)
	(text_box "- UVLO must be greater than 1.2V for start-up to begin\n- Slope compenssation programmed by resistor between it and GND\n- OPT enables SYNCOUT operation (GND in opur case as not needed)\n\nCSN : Inverting input of current sense amp\nCSP : Non-inverting input of current sense amp\n- Current sense amplifer is a differential amplifer that measures voltage across a small current sense resistor. From that tiny voltage it determines how much current is flowing through the converter.\n- Needed to know inductor current so we can regulate output, provide current limiting, protect from overload, and improve transient response.\n- Current sense points each receive a different voltage and thus tell use voltage across R_Sense which we use to interpret current."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 163.322 180.086 0)
		(size 83.566 28.702)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.1588)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "bdc5882e-9cf2-4587-8d8b-a5b097790641")
	)
	(text_box "LM5122 Rating\nVCC : 9V to 20V\nVIN, CSP, CSN : -0.3V to 75V\nBST, FB : -0.3V to 15V\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 175.006 89.662 0)
		(size 32.258 9.652)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.1588)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "bde21715-5649-499d-af73-e7b27109f8c3")
	)
	(text_box "Boost converters begins with an input voltage applied across the inductor. When the PWM-controlled switch (MIC2288YD5) turns ON, the inductor is connected to ground, allowing current through the inductor to increase linearly and energy to be stored in its magnetic field. During this time, the output capacitor supplies the load as diode is reverse-biased. When the PWM switch turns OFF, the inductor resists the sudden decrease in current by collapsing its magnetic field and generating a voltage that adds to the input voltage. This higher voltage forward-biases the diode, allowing current to flow into the output capacitor and load. The capacitor stores this energy and smooths the output voltage, resulting in an output voltage greater than the input voltage."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 22.098 79.756 0)
		(size 118.364 16.002)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "bfd40865-4549-4de2-9fd3-6b32575f0ae7")
	)
	(text_box "We could use a similar approach to that of our buck converter, but timing becomes far harder as we need to worry more about reverse current through the MOSFET. Diodes only conduct in one direction, but MOSFETs are bi-directional. Is inductor current falls to zero during secondary MOSFETs on cycle we may see current flow back toward input."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 195.072 65.532 0)
		(size 178.054 5.334)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.1588)
				(color 255 33 69 1)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "f4fdfa03-cbd8-445b-9cb5-6a367a7b5fcd")
	)
